{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nGit failure strings are classified at five sites with different verdicts for the same text:\n- `src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py:215,221,517` — index-lock and transient-FS markers, retried locally.\n- `src/rebar/_store/push_classify.py:48,61,110` — non-FF / policy-decline / transport markers.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_ref_lock_push.py:68,74` and `_advisory_lock.py:272` — CAS mismatch, terminal.\n- `src/rebar/_store/event_append.py:465` — invalid-object recovery.\n\n`cannot lock ref` receives three different verdicts. The synthetic `git timed out after` result is produced by THREE `_git` shims (`push.py:71`, `sync.py:66`, `event_commit_git.py:64`) and treated as retriable by only one. Bug `unheedful-custodial-bluebottle` was two of these drifting; `wrongful-chemic-squeaker` added the transient retry to `s3_doctor` only, leaving `sync` and `push_recovery` merges without it.\n\n## Approach\nAdd `src/rebar/_store/git_outcome.py`: one marker registry and `classify(result, *, operation) -> GitOutcome` with kinds `LOCK, TRANSIENT_FS, TRANSPORT, NON_FF, CAS_MISMATCH, POLICY_DECLINE, DIRTY_WD, UNMERGED, INVALID_OBJECT, FATAL` (ten), the subtractive \"policy wins over non-FF\" rule applied once, and `run_git(..., retry=RetryPolicy)` folding the timeout shims. Existing predicates become lookups; marker tables move, not change. Guard test: the marker strings (`index.lock`, `non-fast-forward`, `cannot lock ref`, `could not parse head`) appear in `src/rebar` only in `git_outcome.py` (`# git-marker-ok:` escape).\n\n## Guard construction\nFollow the guard-test construction rule in the parent epic `airborne-wellloved-kingfisher`: owner module exempt, match the constituent conjunction or AST shape named in Approach (or the definition count where no distinctive atom exists), `# <construct>-ok: <reason>` escape marker, and behaviour pinned only by the named existing tests.\n\n## Constraint: the classifier signature\n`classify(result, *, operation) -> GitOutcome`, where `result` carries the FULL git outcome — `args`, `returncode`, `stdout`, `stderr` — not just stderr text. Two load-bearing reasons:\n\n1. **A marker alone is ambiguous.** The same string means a retriable local lock, a non-CAS push reject, or a held advisory lock depending on the operation. The registry is keyed `(marker, operation)`, not `marker`.\n2. **Some verdicts are not marker-based at all.** `_advisory_lock._is_cas_mismatch` discriminates on command shape and exit code: `\"update-ref\" in args and ref_name in args`, then `returncode == 128` OR (`returncode == 1` and `\"cannot lock ref\" in stderr`). A `(marker, operation) -> kind` TABLE cannot express that. The registry therefore admits two entry kinds: a **marker row** (common case) and a **structural predicate** registered for an operation, receiving the whole `result`. `_is_cas_mismatch` MOVES into the registry as the `ref-cas` predicate **verbatim** — its logic is not rewritten — and `_ref_lock` keeps importing the same discriminator so its `acquire/release/steal/renew` seams are untouched.\n\nThis section is authoritative over any earlier `classify(result)` wording.\n\n## Constraint: `cannot lock ref` is operation-dependent and MUST NOT be merged\nIt carries three deliberately different, bug-hardened verdicts:\n- `gitutil._is_git_lock_error` — a LOCAL ref-lock conflict, ridden out (retriable).\n- `_ref_lock_push.py:68` — present in `PUSH_REJECT_MARKERS` but explicitly NOT a lease mismatch: **bug 4afc** established that only `stale info` is the `--force-with-lease` signal, because `cannot lock ref` also covers hook declines, rate limits, server errors and remote ref.lock contention. **Bug ebee** added `fatal error in commit_refs` to the same non-CAS set.\n- `_advisory_lock.py:305` — with exit 128 or exit 1, means the advisory lock is HELD (terminal).\n\nCollapsing these to one kind would re-open 4afc and ebee. The divergence is PRESERVED, not merged; the guard proves the marker strings live in one module, not that they carry one verdict.\n\n## Constraint: the tenth kind, INVALID_OBJECT\n`event_append.py:465` `_INVALID_OBJECT_MARKERS = (\"invalid object\", \"error building trees\")`, consumed by `_is_invalid_object_error`, is git `write-tree` refusing a commit because an index entry references an object MISSING from the object DB. Its handler is a **recovery** (rebuild the index and retry), not a terminal failure. Folding it into `FATAL` would delete that recovery — a vanished-object commit would start failing outright instead of self-healing. So the registry carries a tenth kind, `INVALID_OBJECT`, whose action is \"rebuild the index, then retry once\", and `_is_invalid_object_error` becomes a lookup against it exactly as the other predicates do.\n\n## Scope\n`src/rebar/_store/git_outcome.py` (new), `src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py`, `src/rebar/_store/push_classify.py`, `src/rebar/_store/push.py`, `src/rebar/_store/sync.py`, `src/rebar/_store/push_recovery.py`, `src/rebar/_store/event_commit_git.py`, `src/rebar/_store/event_append.py`, `src/rebar/_store/s3_doctor.py`, `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_ref_lock_push.py`, `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_advisory_lock.py`, `tests/unit/store/test_git_outcome.py` (new).\n\n## Testing\nTable-driven test: one captured stderr per kind (from the existing tests' fixtures) maps to exactly one kind. Edge: a stderr carrying both `rejected` and a policy-decline marker classifies POLICY_DECLINE. Pinning: `tests/unit/test_gitutil_transient_write_retry.py`, `tests/unit/test_lock_retry_zebrafish.py`, `tests/unit/test_lock_stale_reclaim.py`, `tests/unit/test_push_rejection_surfacing.py`, `tests/unit/test_push_policy.py`, `tests/integration/test_s3_doctor*.py`, and the reconciler ref-lock tests pass unchanged.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every current marker string resolves to one kind **for a given operation**, and the kind-to-action mapping at each caller is unchanged; checked by the table test plus the pinning suites.\n- [ ] `sync._union_merge` and `push_recovery` merges retry TRANSIENT_FS faults through the shared `run_git`; checked by a new test injecting `could not parse head` once into a reconverge merge.\n- [ ] The **three** `_git` synthetic-timeout shims (`push.py:71`, `sync.py:66`, `event_commit_git.py:64`) are deleted and their callers use the shared `run_git`; checked by the guard asserting exactly one definition of the synthetic-timeout construct under `src/rebar`, owner (`git_outcome.py`) exempt — NOT by a bare `grep -rn \"timed out after\" src/rebar/_store`, which still matches legitimately inside the shared `run_git` after a correct implementation.\n- [ ] `cannot lock ref` keeps its three distinct verdicts; checked by a table test asserting each operation's verdict for that one string.\n- [ ] `_advisory_lock._is_cas_mismatch`'s exit-code + command-shape discrimination is preserved exactly (exit 128, or exit 1 with `cannot lock ref`, only for an `update-ref` naming the ref); checked by a test driving all four combinations through the registry.\n- [ ] `push_recovery.py` is in Scope and its merges retry TRANSIENT_FS through the shared `run_git`; checked by the AC-2 injection test.\n- [ ] `INVALID_OBJECT` is a distinct kind whose action remains index-rebuild-then-retry (NOT folded into FATAL); checked by a test injecting `invalid object` into an event append and asserting the rebuild path still runs and the append then succeeds."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787370369886641001,"uuid":"71fa35d7-45b2-4f60-b602-72b191da937b"}